                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cbe905db-b81e-3b4e-8a1d-97b4b2acc4aa",
                                        "entry_id": "b2ead594-3445-32ef-9af5-b32477d410f4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To represent with numbers after a decimal point."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c6d26448-882d-484c-9ed9-1513df56adce",
                                                "sense_id": "cbe905db-b81e-3b4e-8a1d-97b4b2acc4aa",
                                                "text": "Single- decimaled entries, often originating in Whiting (Wh) or Wilson (OW; see p. 23, fn.3), are from SPL. To save space, examples cited in SPL are normally not repeated (examples therefore begin with \"Add:\"). Double- decimaled entries, again often based on Wh or OW, are \"new.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aabd0f69-0593-41d5-8510-454f4ff79c9e",
                                                "sense_id": "cbe905db-b81e-3b4e-8a1d-97b4b2acc4aa",
                                                "text": "simplified, substantively interpretable versions of the optimal (but many-decimaled) linear combinations of your original variables"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0734ca57-b614-4394-ae38-4ce0245cc318",
                                                "sense_id": "cbe905db-b81e-3b4e-8a1d-97b4b2acc4aa",
                                                "text": "So naturally, 3/14 – the 14th of March – is celebrated as “Pi Day.” And since 3.14 is the beginning, not the end of Pi – it can be decimaled on out to infinity – and since the next two numbers are 1 and 5 voila! today’s date 3/14/15 is really special."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
